$DIR/main.p:8:9: cannot return n as an interface: an ordinary interface only borrows the box, which is dropped when f returns
    n points into the object n refers to, which n keeps alive in f. n (declared
    at main.p:7) is an owned[N]: the object it refers to lives only while n
    holds it, and is dropped when n is dropped at the end of its scope in f, or
    released when it is the last reference. A pointer into the object, or an
    interface that lends it, is a view that may not outlive n. Converting n to
    an ordinary interface lends it: the interface points at the object, and n
    still owns it.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the box itself, changing the result type to owned[N]:
        return n
    Or declare the interface as an owning interface, which takes ownership of
    what is converted into it (language spec section 18.5):
        type I owner interface {
            ...
        }
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
